ctor, and television producer best known for his science fiction and techno-thriller…</span>\n                </a>\n            \n        </div>\n        \n\n        <div class=\"topic-content\">\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Created By\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/jurassic-park/michael-crichton\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Michael Crichton\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Michael Crichton created Jurassic Park.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n        </div>\n    </details>\n</section>\n\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"did-you-know\">\n    <h2>Did you know?</h2>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Jurassic Park is also referenced as JP, Jurassic Park franchise.</li><li>Michael Crichton created Jurassic Park.</li><li>The 1993 film defines Jurassic Park.</li><li>Isla Nublar hosts the parks.</li><li>The novel begins the Jurassic Park franchise.</li><li>Evidence around Jurassic Park is classified as fiction on Ton-o-Lore.</li>\n    </ul>\n</section>","overview":"<section class=\"overview\">\n    <h2>What is Jurassic Park?</h2>\n    \n    <p>\n        The Jurassic Park franchise is a series of books, films and video games centering on the attempt to create a theme park of cloned dinosaurs. It began in 1990 when Universal Studios bought the rights to the novel by <a href=\"/jurassic-park/michael-crichton\" class=\"entity-link\">Michael Crichton</a> before it was even published.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The book was very successful, as was the 1993 film adaptation which led to six sequels with the second being an adaptation of the book&#039;s sequel, although the third, fourth, fifth, sixth, and seventh films were not based on a novel, as the previous two films were. The software developers Ocean Software, BlueSky Software and Sega of America have had the rights to developing video games since the 1993 film, and numerous games have been produced.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        <a href=\"/jurassic-park/michael-crichton\" class=\"entity-link\">Michael Crichton</a> originally conceived a screenplay around a pterodactyl being cloned from fossil DNA. After wrestling with this idea for a while, he came up with Jurassic Park. Steven Spielberg learned of the novel in October 1989 while he and Crichton were discussing a screenplay that would become the TV series ER. Before the book was published, Crichton put up a non-negotiable fee for $1.5 million as well as a substantial percentage of the gross. Warner Bros., Tim Burton, Columbia Tristar, Richard Donner, 20th Century Fox and Joe Dante also bid for the rights, Universal further paid Crichton $500,000 to adapt his own novel, but Universal eventually acquired them in May 1990 for Spielberg. Universal desperately needed money to keep their company alive, and partially succeeded with Jurassic Park, as it became a critical and commercial success.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        After Jurassic Park was released to home video, Crichton was pressured from many sources for a sequel novel. Crichton declined all offers until Spielberg himself told him that he would direct the sequel, if one would ever occur. Production then began almost immediately. After the novel was published in 1995, <a href=\"/jurassic-park/the-lost-world\" class=\"entity-link\">The Lost World: Jurassic Park</a> began production in September 1996.\n    </p>\n    \n    <p class=\"also-known-as\">\n        <strong>Also known as:</strong>\n        JP, Jurassic Park franchise.\n    </p>\n\n</section>","word_count":1034,"uniqueness_score":"1.0000"},"entity":{"id":"6ef4b887-a387-4ed8-a3fd-373d689f351a","name":"Jurassic Park","slug":"jurassic-park","type":"topic","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic","short_description":"The Jurassic Park franchise is a series of books, films and video games centering on the attempt to create a theme park of cloned dinosaurs. It began in 1990 when Universal Studios bought the rights to the novel by…","description":"The Jurassic Park franchise is a series of books, films and video games centering on the attempt to create a theme park of cloned dinosaurs. It began in 1990 when Universal Studios bought the rights to the novel by Michael Crichton before it was even published.\n\nThe book was very successful, as was the 1993 film adaptation which led to six sequels with the second being an adaptation of the book's sequel, although the third, fourth, fifth, sixth, and seventh films were not based on a novel, as the previous two films were. The software developers Ocean Software, BlueSky Software and Sega of America have had the rights to developing video games since the 1993 film, and numerous games have been produced.\n\nMichael Crichton originally conceived a screenplay around a pterodactyl being cloned from fossil DNA. After wrestling with this idea for a while, he came up with Jurassic Park. Steven Spielberg learned of the novel in October 1989 while he and Crichton were discussing a screenplay that would become the TV series ER. Before the book was published, Crichton put up a non-negotiable fee for $1.5 million as well as a substantial percentage of the gross. Warner Bros., Tim Burton, Columbia Tristar, Richard Donner, 20th Century Fox and Joe Dante also bid for the rights, Universal further paid Crichton $500,000 to adapt his own novel, but Universal eventually acquired them in May 1990 for Spielberg. Universal desperately needed money to keep their company alive, and partially succeeded with Jurassic Park, as it became a critical and commercial success.\n\nAfter Jurassic Park was released to home video, Crichton was pressured from many sources for a sequel novel. Crichton declined all offers until Spielberg himself told him that he would direct the sequel, if one would ever occur. Production then began almost immediately. After the novel was published in 1995, The Lost World: Jurassic Park began production in September 1996.","evidence":"fiction","universe":"jurassic-park","aliases":["JP","Jurassic Park franchise"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":null,"image_source":null,"image_credit":null,"image_license":null,"image_usage":null,"image_alt":null},"connections":[{"id":"96cc11f8-1f97-43d1-8e1e-3abf8ec2d8e7","relationship":"part_of","title":"Part of","explanation":"Part of","strength":"0.9000","discovery_score":"0.8500","direction":"incoming","from_entity_id":"8baa7c4b-3afd-4ce9-bd63-3f1ebeea4884","to_entity_id":"6ef4b887-a387-4ed8-a3fd-373d689f351a","entity_id":"8baa7c4b-3afd-4ce9-bd63-3f1ebeea4884","slug":"badlands-national-park","name":"Badlands National Park","type":"place","short_description":"is a national park of the United States located in southwestern South Dakota. It protects 242,756 acres of sharply eroded buttes and pinnacles, as well as mixed grass prairie. Native Americans were once known to have…","evidence":"fiction","image_url":null,"path":"/jurassic-park/badlands-national-park","kind":"location","type_label":"Location"}],"rabbit_holes":[],"copyright":{"theme":"jurassic-park","title":"Jurassic Park is not owned by Ton-o-Lore.","body":"Jurassic Park, Jurassic World, and related names, characters, and imagery are the property of their respective rights holders, including the estate of Michael Crichton, Universal Pictures, Amblin Entertainment, and other credited rights holders. Ton-o-Lore is an independent, unofficial fan and informational project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by those rights holders. Identification logos and thumbnails, when shown, are low-resolution images used under a fair-use rationale for commentary and identification only — not free or redistributable artwork."},"subject":{"id":"jurassic-park","name":"Jurassic Park","theme":"jurassic-park","path":"/jurassic-park","logo":{"license":"fair_use","usage":"fair_use_identification","url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip